.It Em undo
Reverse a sort run (the most recent one by default).
.Pp
Each moved file is looked for at its recorded destination, then wherever the kernel says its inode is now, then by document ID inside the run's bucket folders. Files are put back with an exclusive rename, so nothing on the Desktop is ever overwritten.

Without RUN_ID, the most recent sort run that is not fully reverted is undone. If an original name is taken, the file comes back as `name (restored).ext`, unless --on-conflict skip is given. A file found at its destination but with a different identity is reported as replaced and left alone, unless --by-path is given. Bucket folders the run created are removed once they are empty. Entries left unresolved can be retried later with `dtidy undo RUN_ID`. Exits 0 only when every entry was restored or already restored, otherwise 1.

EXAMPLES:
  dtidy undo
      Undo the most recent sort run.
  dtidy undo 20261014T190912Z-3f9a0c1d2e4b
      Undo (or retry) one specific run; see `dtidy history` for run IDs.
  dtidy undo --dry-run
      Show where each file was found and what would happen, without changing anything.
